    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d469">

                <pb facs="0640" n="516" sameAs="frus:frus1921v01/pg516"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.102 Ic/28</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Schurman</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1921-09-29"><hi rendition="#italic">September 29,
                        1921</hi></date>.<lb/>[Received November 9.]</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 45</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: With reference to the Department’s
                    telegraphic instruction of August 26, 11 a.m., No. 238, and previous
                    correspondence regarding the Ichang situation, I have the honor to transmit
                    herewith copies of the circular of the Dean of the Diplomatic Corps of August
                    30, 1921, No. 150, which contains the text of a note on the subject which the
                    Dean addressed on that day to the Minister for Foreign Affairs on behalf of the
                    Diplomatic Body, together with the text of a letter, dated August 29th, which
                    Mr. Ruddock addressed to the Dean in pursuance of the Department’s instruction
                    under reference.<anchor n="7" xml:id="d469-f1"/></p>
                <p>There are further enclosed copies of Dean Circular No. 156, of September 24,
                    1921, giving the reply of the Chinese Government to the Dean’s note of August
                        30th.<anchor n="8" xml:id="d469-f2"/> In commenting upon this circular, I
                    have simply noted that I was transmitting the text of Dr. Yen’s <pb facs="0641"
                        n="517" xml:id="pg517"/> communication to my Government for such
                    instructions as it might deem suitable. I accordingly have the honor to request
                    the Department’s views in the premises.</p>
                <p>I have [etc.]</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Jacob Gould
                    Schurman</hi></signed></closer>



                <xi:include href="d469-a1.xml" xpointer="d469-a1"/>
                <xi:include href="d469-a2.xml" xpointer="d469-a2"/>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d469-f1" n="7" xml:id="d469-f1-content">
                <p>Only that part of circular 150 containing the note of Aug. 30 addressed by the
                    dean to the Chinese Minister of Foreign Affairs, has been printed.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d469-f2" n="8" xml:id="d469-f2-content">
                <p>Only that part of circular 156 containing the reply of the Chinese Government has
                    been printed.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
